      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "138 elderly ambulatory visitors to a senior citizens club were asked to fill in a standardized questionnaire dealing with symptom pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Some 88% of those participating indicated that they experienced pain at least occasionally. 50% admitted to experiencing pain on the day of the investigation. For the most part, pain occurred during the day and most commonly involved joints and legs. A total of 75% claimed that they would, in principle, use analgesics. Of these, 80% admitted to taking painkillers at least once a week, while 39% reported daily usage. A clear preference was shown for tablets and capsules or suppositories over ointments and intramuscular injections, with drops bringing up the rear of the popularity list."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The results of the investigation reveal the frequency of pain in the elderly, and underscore the importance of providing appropriate pain treatment."
        }
      ]},
